  <h2>Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Architects and urban planners need diverse, realistic 3D building geometries early in the design cycle: site planning, regulatory exploration, and trade-off analysis between cost, carbon, and floor area. Manual mass-modelling is slow, and optimising across competing objectives by hand doesn't scale beyond a handful of variants.
  </p>
  <p>
    EarlyBuild GmbH wanted a generator that could synthesise plausible building geometries on demand <em>and</em> let architects walk the Pareto front of the objectives that actually matter on a German project: DIN 276 construction cost, 50-year operating cost, embodied + operational CO₂, and floor area.
  </p>

  <h2>Architecture</h2>
  <p>EarlyBuild Studio is two systems: an inference backend and a dashboard.</p>
  <h3>Backend (Flask + PyTorch + pymoo)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>VAE-GNN inference engine:</strong> V1 GATConv, 136,004 parameters, trained on 1.1M building samples. Encoder produces a latent representation; GNN layers with spatial attention capture inter-component relationships; four decoder heads produce geometry, height, building type, and vertices.</li>
    <li><strong>NSGA-III optimisation pipeline:</strong> pymoo runs multi-objective optimisation across DIN 276 construction cost, 50-year operating cost, embodied + operational CO₂, and floor area. Coefficients are <strong>runtime-overridable</strong> via YAML or env vars so clients can tune the cost model without redeploying.</li>
    <li><strong>AI4AD preference learning:</strong> a decision-tree classifier trains on user-saved designs to learn implicit aesthetic and programmatic preferences, then biases generation toward what this user has historically liked.</li>
    <li><strong>Output:</strong> 8 building types (residential, commercial, industrial, office, retail, mixed, public, other), 3 complexity levels, configurable generation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Dashboard (React 19 + TypeScript + Vite)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Three.js</strong> for the interactive 3D viewer: building geometry rendered with material selection driven by building type.</li>
    <li><strong>Mapbox GL JS</strong> for the urban context: buildings extruded onto a real basemap so planners see massing in situ.</li>
    <li><strong>Recharts</strong> for the Pareto-front explorer: architects scrub across the trade-off surface and click into specific solutions.</li>
    <li><strong>Unity3D integration</strong> via a Typogen formatter (128×128 grid constraints, automated material selection) so the same geometry can flow into VR/AR tooling downstream.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Approach</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>VAE for diversity, GNN for structure.</strong> A pure VAE produces plausible-looking building shells but can't enforce inter-component relationships. The GNN layers with spatial attention make the geometry actually behave like a building, with components relating to each other consistently rather than floating independently.</li>
    <li><strong>NSGA-III over NSGA-II.</strong> Four objectives (cost, energy, carbon, area) is in the regime where NSGA-III's reference-point machinery genuinely helps: the Pareto front isn't a 2D curve, and architects need a structured way to explore it.</li>
    <li><strong>Runtime-overridable cost model.</strong> DIN 276 categories shift between projects (different regions, different client appetite for sustainability premium). Coefficients live in YAML and can be patched via env var so a client tweak doesn't require a release.</li>
    <li><strong>Test surface.</strong> 17 test cases covering generation determinism, optimisation convergence and the API contract. Without these the model becomes an unverifiable black box for the architect using it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <p>
    Live demo at <a href="https://earlybuild.adityav.au" target="_blank" rel="noopener">earlybuild.adityav.au</a>: the same EarlyBuild Studio shipped to the client, kept live as a portfolio surface. Forms the academic basis for the RMIT capstone project and underwrites the consulting relationship with EarlyBuild GmbH.
  </p>

